# ComposeUX `device_monitor` — Capability-Driven Composition [SPEC:CIO-UX-DEVMON]

> Static layout is author-driven: a human places every widget. `device_monitor` inverts it — the **device's advertised capabilities** drive which widgets appear. Point it at a device; it discovers what that device publishes and can do, and stamps the right component for each capability. A box that can be power-cycled grows a Power Cycle button; one that reports disk, CPU temp, and uptime grows a bar, a gauge, and a readout — automatically, and identically whether the box is a MinnowBoard, an RPi, or an ESP32. This is the runtime counterpart to spec 14: components are the vocabulary, `device_monitor` is the binder that selects from it based on what the device says about itself.

---

## [DM-1] Why: the surface should follow the device

In a fleet, devices are heterogeneous and change over time — fleetnode loses its disk and comes back re-provisioned; a new node joins; an ESP32 exposes temperature but no power control. Hand-authoring a bespoke panel per device does not scale and goes stale the moment hardware changes. `device_monitor` makes the panel a **function of the device's live self-description**: no capability, no widget; new capability, new widget; capability gone (device dark), widget reflects absence. The author declares *intent* ("monitor fleetnode, using these components for these capability kinds"), not a fixed widget list.

> test: If adding a capability to a device requires editing the surface config to see it, device_monitor has failed its purpose.

---

## [DM-2] Capabilities are ADVERTISED, never sniffed

The load-bearing rule. `device_monitor` must not guess "`*_temp_c` looks like a temperature." A `json` temperature and a `json` uptime are indistinguishable by value. So a device **advertises a capability descriptor** per namespace as part of ComposeIO discovery — small, semantic, ≤256 bytes (DP-CIO-18), one per namespace on join:

```yaml
# published by the device's adapter as discovery metadata for each namespace it owns
capability:
  namespace: fleetnode/cpu_temp_c
  kind: temperature          # SEMANTIC role, not a value guess
  unit: celsius
  range: {min: 0, max: 100}
  writable: false            # read-only telemetry
# a control capability:
capability:
  namespace: fleetnode/power
  kind: power_cycle
  writable: true
  tool: fleetpower.power_cycle   # the mcp_action this capability maps to (spec 13)
  args_template: {target: fleetnode}
```

`kind` is the semantic hinge (`temperature | percent | bytes_free | uptime | throughput | power_cycle | reboot | link_status | …`). This reuses ComposeIO's principle that **the edge closes the semantic gap** (DP): the device says *what a value means*, not just its number — so the surface (or an agent) can render it without domain heuristics. Devices too dumb to self-describe get a static adapter that advertises on their behalf (same pattern as the Phase-1 console adapter).

---

## [DM-3] The widget: intent + a capability→component map

```yaml
- type: device_monitor
  device: fleetnode                      # namespace prefix / device id to discover
  label: "fleetnode"
  capability_map:                     # capability kind → which component to stamp (spec 14 vocabulary)
    - {match: {kind: temperature}, use: temp-gauge,    params: {label: "{{ns.label}}", ns: "{{ns.namespace}}", max: "{{ns.range.max}}"}}
    - {match: {kind: percent},     use: util-gauge,    params: {label: "{{ns.label}}", ns: "{{ns.namespace}}"}}
    - {match: {kind: bytes_free},  use: disk-bar,      params: {label: "{{ns.label}}", ns: "{{ns.namespace}}"}}
    - {match: {kind: uptime},      use: uptime-readout,params: {ns: "{{ns.namespace}}"}}
    - {match: {kind: power_cycle}, use: command-button,params: {label: "Power Cycle", tool: "{{ns.tool}}", args: "{{ns.args_template}}", confirm: true}}
  fallback: {use: generic-readout, params: {ns: "{{ns.namespace}}"}}   # advertised but unmapped → generic
```

For each discovered capability, `device_monitor` finds the first `match`, binds the component's params from the **capability descriptor** (`{{ns.*}}` reads descriptor fields), and stamps it via spec 14's `_instantiate(name, params)`. The Power Cycle case falls straight out: a `power_cycle` capability maps to `command-button` with the descriptor's `tool`/`args` — the exact button we hand-built in spec 13, now materialized from advertisement. Unmapped-but-advertised capabilities get the `fallback` (visible, not silently dropped — DM anti-pattern).

---

## [DM-4] Discovery lifecycle — mount, refresh, go-dark

`device_monitor` is a live discovery subscriber, not a one-shot:

- **Join** — capability descriptor arrives → stamp its component, grid it into the container.
- **Update** — value changes flow through the stamped component's ordinary namespace binding (nothing special; it is a normal widget once stamped).
- **Depart / silence** — device stops heartbeating (DP discovery timeout) → the container marks itself **degraded** and its stamped widgets go *stale* (external authority, DP-CIO-19 — never a frozen-but-green lie). fleetnode's disk death is the canonical case: the box pinged but stopped advertising; a `device_monitor` over it would have flipped to degraded on the heartbeat gap, surfacing the fault without anyone reading a log.
- **Re-provision** — fleetnode returns with a fresh card and re-advertises → the container re-composes from scratch. No config edit.

Ordering/idempotency: stamping is keyed by namespace, so a re-announced capability replaces rather than duplicates.

---

## [DM-5] device_monitor is a runtime binder over spec-14 components

The elegance the design was probing for: **one component vocabulary, two instancers.**

| | Author-time (spec 14) | Runtime (spec 15) |
|---|---|---|
| Who stamps | human, in YAML `use:` | `device_monitor`, per discovered capability |
| Params from | literals in the config | capability descriptor fields (`{{ns.*}}`) |
| When | config load | discovery event |
| Same `_instantiate()` | ✅ | ✅ |

Because both go through spec 14's pure `_instantiate(name, params)`, a component authored for hand-placement is *immediately* usable by `device_monitor` with zero change. That two-consumer symmetry is the evidence the component abstraction is at the right level (COMP-7).

---

## [DM-6] Data sources & Prometheus — complementary layers, two bridge modes

`device_monitor` needs values; for a real fleet, many already live in Prometheus. The two systems have **complementary** shapes, and the pairing is load-bearing, not incidental:

- **Prometheus** = pull, time-series, scrape-and-**retain**, and a **vast exporter ecosystem** (node, SNMP, blackbox, IPMI, hundreds of third-party). ComposeIO has, by design, **no sense of history** and no wish to re-integrate the world.
- **ComposeIO** = push, semantic, ≤256-byte, **current** state + coordination + control.

**1. Prometheus is ComposeIO's universal southbound.** Its exporter ecosystem is integration you do not rebuild. Write **one** Prometheus→bus bridge and anything Prometheus can already scrape becomes projectable onto the backplane — you inherit its integration surface instead of writing a ComposeIO emitter per device type. Don't out-integrate Prometheus; **ride it.** It is also the **retention layer** the bus deliberately lacks.

**2. The bridge is a PROJECTION, and the dividing line is cardinality × rate — not number-vs-enum.** A precise current value of a named attribute is a first-class bus citizen: `fleetnode/cpu_temp_c = 61.2` is tiny (a float clears DP-CIO-18 with enormous headroom) and semantic (the namespace names the meaning; the number is its value) — it is exactly what the HUD gauges already consume. What you do *not* do is mirror *every* series at *scrape frequency*, because the bus has no retention — that pumps history-shaped data through a historyless pipe. So: project the **attributes you need** at the **rate a live surface needs them**; leave the full-resolution series in Prometheus. Two modes, chosen per attribute:
   - **Instant-value projection** — a bridge runs a Prometheus **instant query** (`/api/v1/query`) for a selected attribute and publishes the **precise current value** to its namespace. Cadence-driven for a live gauge, or **on-demand** (an mcp_action "refresh", or `device_monitor` asking a capability for its current state). The "I need the exact current state of this attribute on the bus" path — and there is bandwidth to spare.
   - **Derived-fact projection** — a **recording/alert rule** computes a semantic fact (`disk_free/size < 0.1 → pressure`), Alertmanager fires a **webhook**, a thin adapter publishes it. The rule *is* the edge that closes the gap; event-driven, no polling on the bus side. The "tell me when it crosses / changes regime" path.

   **Freshness caveat:** a Prometheus-sourced "current" value is current *as of the last scrape* (seconds old). For disk/CPU/temp/uptime that is plenty; for hard-real-time precise state, source direct from the device, not via Prometheus.

**3. Grafana and `device_monitor` are complementary, not competing.** Grafana answers history/trend/alert; `device_monitor` answers current-state-plus-*act-now*, is agent-authored, and runs on a CYD/ESP32 where Grafana cannot. A capability can carry a `history_url` to its Grafana panel — live control here, deep history one click away.

**4. Concrete win — and the fix for what killed fleetnode:** the soak logged JSONL to fleetnode's microSD for hours and wore it out. Prometheus inverts that — endpoints run `node_exporter` (metrics served **from memory**), Prometheus **scrapes centrally**, the endpoint **writes nothing to its own flash**. Full-resolution soak history lands in retained Prometheus; only current values + the semantic verdict (`soak/phase`, `soak/verdict`) ride the bus to the HUD. Strictly better than jsonl-on-SD, and it removes the wear failure mode outright.

Net: **Prometheus as universal southbound + retention; a projection bridge (instant-value and/or derived-fact) onto the bus; ComposeUX for the live, actionable surface.** ComposeIO doesn't out-integrate Prometheus and doesn't become a TSDB; Prometheus doesn't become the bus.

---

## [DM-7] Security — advertisement is not authority

A device saying "I have a `power_cycle` capability with `tool: fleetpower.power_cycle`" must **not** thereby authorize the surface to call it. All of spec 13's MCPA-5 still holds, and `device_monitor` adds constraints:

- The stamped control still requires the tool to be in the **surface's allowlist**; a device advertising an off-allowlist tool gets its control capability **dropped (or shown disabled)**, not honored.
- `confirm` for destructive stamped controls is enforced by the component (`command-button` carries `confirm: true`), not by the device's say-so.
- `capability_map` can only reference **components the design ships** — a device cannot inject a novel widget/tool, only trigger the author's pre-declared vocabulary.
- Capability descriptors are **advisory input to a trusted mapping**, never executable content. `device_monitor` is a binder, not an interpreter of device-supplied code.

> test: If a malicious/compromised device can make the surface invoke a tool the author did not allowlist, DM-7 has failed.

---

## Derived constraints

| Constraint | Follows from | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| Widgets stamped only from ADVERTISED capabilities | DM-2 | No value-sniffing; edge closes the semantic gap |
| Capability descriptor ≤256 bytes, one per namespace | DM-2, DP-CIO-18 | Fits the bus; streamed on discovery |
| device_monitor reuses spec-14 `_instantiate()` | DM-5 | One vocabulary, author-time + runtime |
| Go-dark → degraded/stale, never frozen-green | DM-4, DP-CIO-19 | External authority; faults are visible |
| Project SELECTED current values / derived facts, not all series at scrape rate | DM-6 | Cost is cardinality × rate; the bus has no retention |
| Prometheus is the universal southbound + retention; bridge, don't re-integrate | DM-6 | Inherit its exporter ecosystem; bus stays current-state-only |
| Advertised tool still gated by surface allowlist | DM-7, MCPA-5 | Advertisement ≠ authority |

## Anti-patterns

| Anti-pattern | Violates | Do instead |
|---|---|---|
| Infer widget from value shape/name | DM-2 | Require an advertised `kind` |
| Honor a device-advertised off-allowlist tool | DM-7 | Drop/disable; only ship-declared components |
| Mirror every Prometheus series at scrape rate onto the bus | DM-6 | Project selected attributes (instant-value or derived-fact) at the rate the surface needs |
| Write a bespoke ComposeIO emitter per device type | DM-6 | Bridge once from Prometheus; inherit its exporter ecosystem |
| Reimplement Grafana history in ComposeUX | DM-6 | Link `history_url`; keep device_monitor live-only |
| Silently drop an unmapped capability | DM-3 | `fallback` component (visible) |
| Keep last value green after device goes dark | DM-4 | Mark degraded/stale on heartbeat gap |

---

**Version**: 0.1 · **Created**: 2026-07-05 · **Status**: Draft — design established as the runtime test of spec 14. Depends on: a capability-descriptor extension to ComposeIO discovery (DM-2) and the fleet540/node adapters advertising `kind`. Build order: components (14, implementing now) → capability advertisement in the node adapter → device_monitor renderer. Prometheus bridge (DM-6) is an independent adapter, buildable in parallel and the recommended replacement for jsonl-on-SD soak logging.
